.TH UUDECODE 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
uudecode \- decode a uuencoded or base64 file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uudecode
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.RI [ infile ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uudecode
extracts one file from text made by
.BR uuencode (1),
in either the traditional uuencode format or base64.
With no
.IR infile ,
or when it is
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
It skips lines until it finds a header line, which has one of the forms
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\fBbegin\fR \fImode\fR \fIname\fR
\fBbegin\-base64\fR \fImode\fR \fIname\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
The header must start a line, and the keywords are lowercase and
followed by a space.
The first form introduces uuencoded data, and the second base64 data.
The
.I mode
is not checked, so any line that starts with
.B begin
and a space is a header, even ordinary text such as
.BR "begin the work now" ,
which names the file
.IR "work now" .
Text before the header, such as the headers of a mail message, is
ignored, and so is everything after the end of the data.
Only the first file in the input is decoded.
.PP
The decoded bytes are written to the file called
.IR name ,
or to the file named by
.BR \-o .
The
.I name
is everything after the first space that follows
.IR mode ,
with white space removed from both ends, so it may contain spaces.
The
.I mode
is ignored; see
.BR NOTES .
A name of
.B \-
or
.B /dev/stdout
selects standard output.
Any other file is created, or emptied if it exists, as soon as the header
is read.
The directories in its path must already exist.
.PP
If the input has no header, or the header has no name and
.B \-o
is not given,
.B uudecode
fails with
.BR "no \(aqbegin\(aq line" .
.SS Uuencode data
Each line after the header encodes up to 45 bytes.
Its first character is 32 plus the number of bytes.
The rest are groups of 4 characters that each encode 3 bytes, 6 bits per
character, as 32 plus the 6-bit value.
A backquote or a space decodes as zero, so either may pad the last
group.
.PP
Extra characters at the end of a line are ignored.
A line too short for its byte count fails with
.BR "short file" ,
and otherwise a count above 45 fails with
.BR "line too long" .
A line with a count of zero, such as the backquote line that
.B uuencode
writes before the end line, is skipped.
The data ends at a line that is exactly
.BR end .
Lines may end with LF or CR LF.
A blank line in the data, or the end of the input before the end line,
fails with
.BR "short file" .
.SS Base64 data
The data uses the standard base64 alphabet: letters, digits,
.BR + ,
and
.BR / ,
with
.B =
as padding.
Characters outside the alphabet, including white space, are skipped.
The data ends at the end of the input, or earlier at
.B ====
standing alone between white space, anywhere on a line, which
.B uuencode \-m
writes as its last line.
If the input ends in the middle of a group of 4 characters,
.B uudecode
writes what it has decoded and fails with
.BR "truncated input" .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may follow
.IR infile ,
unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set, and the argument of
.B \-o
may be attached, as in
.BR \-ofile .
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BI "\-o " outfile
Write the decoded data to
.I outfile
instead of the name in the header.
The header is still required, but its name may be missing.
An
.I outfile
of
.B \-
or
.B /dev/stdout
selects standard output.
If given more than once, the last
.B \-o
is used.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must precede
.IR infile .
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line,
.I infile
or the output file could not be opened, no usable header was found, or
the data is malformed or truncated.
A usage error prints the usage on standard error, and any other failure
prints a message there.
.TP
.B 141
The output is a pipe whose reader has exited.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decode a file into the name stored in it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uudecode archive.uu
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode an attachment from a saved mail message, into a name of your
choosing.
The text around the attachment is ignored:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uudecode \-o program.exe message.eml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode to a pipe:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uudecode \-o \- archive.uu | xxd | head
.EE
.RE
.PP
Encode a file and decode it again:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ uuencode data.bin data.bin >data.uu
$ uudecode \-o copy.bin data.uu
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from other
.B uudecode
programs:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-o
is the only option, and
.B \-\-help
the only long option.
.IP \(bu 2
At most one
.I infile
is accepted.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.I mode
in the header is ignored.
busybox-w32 does not apply it, so the file gets the default permissions
of a new file.
.PP
The
.I name
in the header is used as it stands.
An absolute path, a drive letter, backslashes, and
.B ..\&
components are all honored, so decoding an untrusted file can overwrite
any file that you can write.
Use
.B \-o
for input that you do not trust.
.PP
The output file is created before any data is decoded, and it is not
removed on failure.
After an error it holds what was decoded before the error.
Errors writing the output are not detected, except that a pipe whose
reader has exited ends the program with status 141.
.PP
Input and output are binary: there is no newline translation, and the
CR of a CR LF line end is removed from the header and from uuencoded
data lines, and is white space in base64 data.
.PP
When the output is a console, the decoded data passes through the
busybox-w32 console layer as text, so binary data does not display
faithfully.
Redirect it to a file or a pipe.
.PP
.I infile
and
.I outfile
may be Windows paths, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
busybox-w32 device names such as
.I /dev/null
and
.IR /dev/stderr .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR base64 (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR uuencode (1)
